    "turns": [
        {
            "role": "A",
            "text": "Behind the technical name GAT, what intuitive problem were researchers confronting?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial-hook"
        },
        {
            "role": "B",
            "text": "GAT replaced fixed graph aggregation weights with learned attention over neighbors. This let each node decide which neighbors mattered most for its representation.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-graph-attention-networks"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.historical-background"
        },
        {
            "role": "A",
            "text": "That sounds like a promising direction. What mechanism actually makes it work?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial"
        },
        {
            "role": "B",
            "text": "A node scores its neighbors, normalizes attention weights, and combines their features into an updated node state.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-graph-attention-networks"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.core-idea"
        },
        {
            "role": "A",
            "text": "Why did the idea outlive one paper or experiment? What did later researchers actually inherit?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial"
        },
        {
            "role": "B",
            "text": "Experts generally treat GAT as a defining attention-based graph model. Its long-term legacy is the idea that graph neighborhoods can be weighted dynamically instead of only by graph degree or fixed normalization.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-graph-attention-networks"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.long-term-legacy"
        },
        {
            "role": "SUMMARY",
            "text": "GAT mattered not only as a result, but as a method that later researchers could test, adapt, and extend.",
